[SourceNote "Main branch follows the user's requested 8.h3 instead of the common 8.Be3 after ...c5. Svidler and Naroditsky video transcripts supply the Grunfeld plan language; GM Neil McDonald/ChessPublishing supplies the exact h3 tabiya and its strategic purpose."]

1. Nf3 Nf6
2. d4 g6
3. c4 d5
4. Nc3 Bg7
5. cxd5 Nxd5
6. e4 Nxc3
7. bxc3 c5
{Naroditsky's core Grunfeld lesson is that Black must attack White's pawn center with pawn breaks and piece pressure; `...c5` is the main strike against d4.}
8. h3
{This is the requested repertoire branch. The point, as emphasized in the h3 theory notes, is to stop the easy `...Bg4` pin and ask whether Black's light-squared bishop can find a useful job. It is quieter than `Be3`, but it gives you a different practical game.}
(8. Be3
{Old weak-deck bridge only. If you choose the common line, you are back in the Kramnik `Be3/Qd2/Rc1` structures rather than this h3 repertoire.}
8... Nc6
9. Qd2 Qa5
10. Rc1 cxd4
11. cxd4 Qxd2+
12. Kxd2 O-O
13. d5 Rd8
14. Bb5
{The old deck's idea is to push d5 and develop with tempo, but Black's queenless counterplay with `...Rd8`, `...e6`, `...f5`, and the c-file remains the thing you must study if you return to `Be3`.})
8... O-O
{The most natural continuation. Black keeps the right to choose between central pressure, queen checks, and the `...Bf5` equalizer.}
(8... Nc6
{A direct equalizing attempt: Black puts pressure on d4 before castling.}
9. Be2
{Keep development simple. Do not drift into slow pawn-grabbing while Black attacks the center.}
9... cxd4
10. cxd4 O-O
11. Be3 Bf5
{Same critical bishop move as the Svidler-style line below. White has achieved the h3 setup, but Black can still ask concrete questions.})
9. Be2
{Svidler's course notes describe this as the sensible way to continue: develop, prepare to castle, and keep the d4/e4 center supported.}
9... cxd4
10. cxd4 Nc6
11. Be3
{Now White has the h3 move inserted, so the usual `...Bg4` pin is unavailable. The catch: Black has a different active bishop route.}
11... Bf5
{The critical equalizer from the Svidler material. If you play h3, you must know this move: Black attacks e4 and tries to prove h3 cost White a tempo.}
12. Bc4+
{A practical way to avoid immediately clarifying the e-pawn tension. White checks, gains a tempo, and keeps the center advanced option available.}
(12. exf5
{Playable only if you understand Black's follow-up. Grabbing the bishop gives Black queen activity and an immediate target on d4.}
12... Qa5+
13. Bd2 Qxf5
14. O-O Nxd4
{Black has recovered the pawn, centralized the knight, and will aim for `...Be6`/`...Bd5` or pressure on the c-file.})
12... Kh8
13. e5
{The ambitious White idea: preserve the big center and gain kingside space before Black fully coordinates.}
13... b5
{Black challenges the bishop and starts queenside counterplay. Svidler's warning is that the h3 line is not a free pass; Black can still be very active.}
14. Bb3 a5
15. O-O a4
16. Bc2
{Repertoire summary: choose `8.h3` to rule out the easy `...Bg4` pin, then develop with `Be2`, castle, and keep the center mobile. If Black knows `...Bf5`, do not autopilot; answer concretely with checks, e5 ideas, or the prepared exf5 endgame.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Grunfeld h3 Plans and Anti-Plans"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l6IwRWbovRU"]
[Date "2021.10.13"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Plan reminders"]
[Black "Grunfeld counterplay"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky and GM-source theory"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Grunfeld Defense: Exchange, h3"]
[SourceTitle "Master Class | Grunfeld Defense | Chess Speedrun | Grandmaster Naroditsky"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l6IwRWbovRU"]

1. d4 Nf6
2. c4 g6
3. Nc3 d5
4. cxd5 Nxd5
5. e4 Nxc3
6. bxc3 Bg7
7. Nf3 c5
8. h3
{Same tabiya without the `1.Nf3` move order. Your practical checklist: prevent `...Bg4`, finish development, and keep asking whether Black has really damaged the d4/e4 center.}
8... O-O
9. Be2 Nc6
10. Be3
{If Black delays `...cxd4`, you can develop first.}
10... Qa5
{A standard Grunfeld poke: queen pressure on c3/d4 and the a1-rook diagonal.}
11. Qd2
{Meet queen pressure by defending c3 and keeping castling available.}
11... Rd8
{Black piles up on d4.}
12. O-O cxd4
13. cxd4 Qxd2
(14. Qxd2
{Avoid if it leaves d4 loose or gives Black easy piece activity. In Grunfeld structures, the queen trade is only good when the d-pawn survives with support.})
14. Nxd2
{A quieter route: trade queens only when your pieces remain coordinated. White's center is still the long-term asset, but Black will use the c-file and queenside majority if you let the game become passive.}
14... Nxd4
15. Bxd4 Bxd4
16. Rab1
{Remember the Naroditsky principle from the Grunfeld videos: Black's play is built around attacking the center. As White, every trade should be judged by whether d4/e4 stay alive and whether your rooks get active before Black's queenside majority starts rolling.} *
